# What this proves, and what it does not:
#
# [V] GainKernelRGBA16FHalf4: 162 shapes, 0 halves wrong. Bit equality against a
#     reference that does the same float multiply and the same single
#     round-to-nearest-even conversion - no tolerance, because two halves
#     multiply exactly in float.
# [V] GainKernelRGBA8: 164 cases, 0 bytes wrong. 162 shapes (the measured
#     frames at a byte pitch, widths 1-33, heights 1/2/3/5, pitches 0/1/3 bytes
#     of padding, one negative pitch) plus the identity ramp and the saturation
#     case.
# [V] byte arithmetic: gain 1.0 returns all 256 code values unchanged, and
#     200/128/1/255 at gain 8 becomes 255/255/8/255 - saturation, not the
#     1600 & 0xff = 64 wrap.
# [V] addressing: an 8-bit pixel is 4 bytes, so odd pitches and odd offsets are
#     legal and are exercised; a byte buffer cannot be identified by its address,
#     the depth has to come from kOfxImageEffectPropPixelDepth.
# [V] the tie rule is visible: with the negative-pitch reference written as
#     lround instead of nearbyint, the case reported 35 of 340 bytes wrong,
#     because gain 0.5 on odd bytes puts every product exactly on .5 and the two
#     rounding rules differ by one LSB there. Both sides were "correct" under
#     their own rule; the kernel's rule is __float2int_rn, so the reference has
#     to use nearbyint.
# [U] every device number. There is no nvcc on this cell and no GPU: the kernels
#     were never compiled to PTX or executed by a driver, so nothing here says
#     anything about launch cost, bandwidth or half/byte throughput.
# [U] a host comparing its own byte render against this one. The quantiser is
#     specified and tested against a float reference, not against Resolve,
#     Nuke or Natron output.
